  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y-2z=-2 \\\\ 5x+y+z=-1 \\\\ -8x+2y+z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -13 & -15 & -11 \\\\ 18 & 22 & 16\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -13 & -15 & -11 \\\\ 18 & 22 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=0,y=4,z=-5.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=0+4+(-5)=-1.$",
